{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes","id":817404,"title":"(ST) How do you handle it when characters stray from Tribal stereotypes?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-01-26T22:03:12-08:00","id":817404,"title":"(ST) How do you handle it when characters stray from Tribal stereotypes?","content":"So when you notice players doing things that generally go against tribal views (Get of Fenris showing mercy, Shadow Lord being compassionate, etc) do you run with it and let it be part of character development? Or do you try to push them back towards the tribal stereotypes?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1250-shadowlost","name":"Shadowlost","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"155"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-26T22:44:33-08:00","id":817423,"title":"","content":"I don't think thats a problem at all. They are personalities first and a specific tribe second as far as I am concerned.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7182-curiel","name":"Curiel","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-26T23:57:05-08:00","id":817438,"title":"","content":"Beyond Curiel's hitting the nail on the head of the core issue...<br /> <br /> What is the character about? Characters don't have to like, or abide, cultural stereotypes. Hell, two major characters are Albrecht (who's main arc in 2e is learning how to reconcile his complete lack of fucks to give about Silver Fang One-True-Leaders bullshit, and being shoved directly into the spotlight to be one of the most powerful leaders in the Garou Nation) and Ethan Heals-the-Past (a chill <i>white</i> Wendigo).<br /> <br /> The important thing, from my perspective, is if the character engages with the Tribe's themes, because that will invariably establish their associations with Tribal stereotypes.<br /> <br /> It's also important to remember that Garou society is partially built around Auspices, which include Ragabash. Every Tribe has built in membership that exists to buck trends, and question the status quo. Tribal tradition obviously has ways to channel new moon inclinations, but that essentially means every Tribe has built in expectations of how to deal with members that stray too far from the party line.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T04:39:11-08:00","id":817519,"title":"","content":"I'd ask the player how they think their actions reflect their tribe (if you're the ST in the game). After all, nothing is as straight forward as it seems and guessing or assuming what is going on in a players or characters head isn't typically a good way to get an actual idea.<br /> <br /> If I saw a shadow lord being kind, i'd figure...there's a long con going on. This is leading to something else. Probably to gain someones favour for later.<br /> If I saw a Get of Fenris showing Mercy, well that would depend on who they showed it too. A wyrm enemy? ok that'd be weird. A gaian opponent that showed great valor and bravery in the past and could be a useful ally in a larger battle to come? well..there's something.<br /> <br /> Personally, i'm all for characters that find new ways and perspectives on living up to their Tribes core beliefs. As long as they do and have considered how they still live up to them under the Tribal Totem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7706-somuchlove","name":"Somuchlove","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7706_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T08:00:39-08:00","id":817599,"title":"","content":"Ive been toldl Im overly strict on the issue... but that was from a newcomer who DINT GRASP How tightly themed the game is... and found it too preachy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T09:03:18-08:00","id":817666,"title":"","content":"I disagree.<br /> Characters were raised to adhere and proved during their rite of passage that they would live by tribal standards.<br /> Sure, lost cubs are not as solidly integrated into the ways of their respective tribes, and sure some tribes have more leeway than others.<br /> <br /> But being of a tribe is a commitment, and more than heritage or just a character option one has to choose.<br /> <br /> EDIT: I disagreed with Heavy arms, but didn't send my post till now...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T09:08:04-08:00","id":817672,"title":"","content":"As said above, it is not a problem.<br /> <br /> For example, Shadow Lord being compassionate. Shadow Lords are one tribe that has made efforts to fix past mistakes they made. That, and there is no reason to not show compassion towards those that deserve it. And not, to those that don't.<br /> <br /> Tribes are very multifaceted, a Silver Fang Galliard can be a knight, a bard, a lorekeeper or a geneticts. What is important is to understand the core of the tribe, and have the character show it in their own way.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T09:16:06-08:00","id":817678,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowlost</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes#post817404\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So when you notice players doing things that generally go against tribal views (Get of Fenris showing mercy, Shadow Lord being compassionate, etc) do you run with it and let it be part of character development? Or do you try to push them back towards the tribal stereotypes?</div> </div> </div> </div>Responding just to this, in a more general sense than just applies to Werewolf, I would say that you can let them know, OOC, how their PC is going against stereotype. Just to make sure they know. Illustrate the stereotype and its source by the actions of members of that group who live up (or down) to it. Do have all NPCs react to the PC's behavior as that NPC would. Most NPCs may be mildly to extremely surprised at behavior that truly flies in the face of their preconceptions based on the stereotypes they expect. Responses could range from variants on the rather insulting &quot;you're a credit to your people&quot; to out-and-out shock and denial to questioning if somebody really IS a member of the group whose stereotype they flout.<br /> <br /> If it would cause tensions, let it cause tensions.<br /> <br /> But don't try to nudge them by telling them they're &quot;playing wrong,&quot; unless you think they're honestly acting from ignorance or ignoring things they IC should be aware of. (If the reason the Glasswalkers look down their noses at the Get of Fenris in your campaign is due to the Get having effectively destroyed several Glasswalker projects that resulted in many Glasswalker kinfolks' deaths, make sure that your PC Glasswalker's player knows that treating the Get like honored guests is basically treating people his tribe views as mass murderers who are likely to flip out at any moment as if they're trustworthy and safe. Fellow Glasswalkers will perceive it as inviting an armed psychopath to your dinner table and sitting him next to your defenseless wife and kids while you go outside to check on the BBQ.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T09:20:44-08:00","id":817680,"title":"","content":"As an ST when I have players make their characters I ask them to list 5 things they like about that tribe and 5 things they do that makes them unique in that tribe's standards. I find it helps players grasp what they really want to play and to see if perhaps another tribe might be suited to what they are looking for. I never really enforce tribal views hardcore but enough to remind people of what they are. Sure that Shadow Lord is nice to you but he just pulled a long con that wll remove the Silver Fang from station and kill off some BSDs in the end.<br /> <br /> (I also asked because I'm currently playing a SL, Philodox (Bringer of Light) who is close to gaining the true love merit (brotherly love not sexual) towards the CoG Philodox. Mainly because she has constantly been the only one to show him kindness and points out that his cleaning up the mess from previous Lords shows he does value his place as a child of Grandfather Thunder. )","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1250-shadowlost","name":"Shadowlost","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"155"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T11:20:36-08:00","id":817784,"title":"","content":"What's supposed to maintain tribal sterotypes in WoD? The totem, or the culture? Because culture is very weak from the point of view of uniformity (think of &quot;the French&quot;; there's a certain stereotype there, with some grounds to it, but finding a French person that violates it is unsurprising).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4851-cheesefondue","name":"Cheesefondue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"275"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T13:23:23-08:00","id":817897,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cheesefondue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=817784#post817784\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What's supposed to maintain tribal sterotypes in WoD? The totem, or the culture? Because culture is very weak from the point of view of uniformity (think of &quot;the French&quot;; there's a certain stereotype there, with some grounds to it, but finding a French person that violates it is unsurprising).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> One idea is tribal reknown bonuses. The original Fianna tribe book had a little blerb about reknown being handed out for living up to tribal ideals. This way you can give a gentle nudge to a player (reward them) if its that important to you. Maybe give extra wisdom to a Shadow Lord who has a great plot, or Honor to a fallen Gaian who a Fenrir crushes posthumously. Players may or may not take the bait, if they don't I don't see the problem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T14:02:48-08:00","id":817941,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=817666#post817666\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I disagree.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure what you're disagreeing with from my post. A character living up to a Tribe's ideals isn't the same thing as a character living up to Tribal stereotypes.<br /> <br /> There's a stereotype of the Black Furies as being aggressive man-haters. A Black Fury that manages to maintain a healthy relationship with her kin husband, and finds time to parent her children (including any boys), is living up to Black Fury ideology on fulfilling the role of the Mother, even if many outside the Tribe would be surprised to see her dedicated to a social role of &quot;wife&quot; given how it often plays into many of the cultural forces the Tribe rails against.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T18:44:48-08:00","id":818123,"title":"","content":"Why bother with character creation rules if every character is required to be created with cookie cutters?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5818-eviltyger","name":"EvilTyger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5818_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T19:21:05-08:00","id":818141,"title":"","content":"I'm a bit confused by this question a character is meant to be the &quot;hero&quot; of a chronicle. Typical behavior of a tribe is a meaningless standard designed to help players not a written in stone commandment that violating sends the conformity squad of the Continuity Bureau of Investigation. The standard is how a typical member of the tribe acts.<br /> All of the tribe books no matter how poorly done greatly expanded what it means to be a tribe member you have Shadow Lords working to atone for the sins of their ancestors, not something you would get from the blurb on the tribe. The Get have the Fangs of Garm which while believing in strength actively work to strengthen ties to other tribes again a direct violation of the sterotype. I have had players that have broken rules of tribes the only thing that determines tribe in game is the tribal totem. It doesn't matter that Albrecht's grandfather banished him and cast him from the tribe why because Falcon said so. It doesn't matter that Evan is white why because Wendigo said so. It doesn't matter that the Red Talon Ahroun lives in a Bone Gnawer Sept. That he sells hallucinogenic mushrooms, that he gives money to the Bone Gnawers to buy food. He is serving Griffon but he lives in a city he is helping apes. His mushrooms may have side effects, he may engineer a drug war, he might have the pack theurge activate the spirits of the mushrooms and bring awareness to his customers of Gaia's plight. <br /> Why let &quot;typical&quot; get in the way of a good story.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/293-lncbill","name":"lncbill","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar293_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-27T19:27:45-08:00","id":818146,"title":"","content":"I am fine if a PC wants to buck the tribal culture, but I always stress that the NPCs will react accordingly. In these kind of cultures, the nail that sticks up gets hammered down. The character may be socially isolated, not considered respectable, looked down upon, and this may have an impact on Renown rewards.<br /> <br /> Some players are fine with this, and even get a kick out of it. To compensate for the in-character burdens, I may boost their XP award for roleplaying, but not the extent other PCs get turned off. However, in experience most players who want to buck tribal culture don't do it to experience hardship and drama. It's because they want to be treated like special snowflakes or even get praised for &quot;reforming&quot; their tribal culture. They get upset when the NPCs don't react the way they imagined. So I am very blunt about it.<br /> <br /> If a character concept is completely out of reason, then no. Also, it is important to remind players that the tribal culture was brainwashed into them as cubs. If they changed when young - which is most of the time - then they should have been indoctrinated quite well. As a Lost Cub, there is more leeway because the personality is already fully formed. If the player is outside the typical tribal culture, it is important to explain why as part of the character backstory. And of course auspice plays a big part - Ragabash can be contrary, a Philodox or Gallaird likely won't.<br /> <br /> I think it is more important the ST portrays the tribes' NPCs approrpriately than that the PCs do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-28T01:04:04-08:00","id":818274,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=817941#post817941\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure what you're disagreeing with from my post.</div> </div> </div> </div>Basically the 2nd paragraph's second sentence.<br /> <br /> My point being, that they have to like and abide the cultural characteristics of their tribe, if not they wouldn't have made it into the tribe. Even Albrecht was a picture book Silver Fang, before he was banished for being to successful. Even Evan-Heals-the-Past tries to live as a Wendigo and adheres to their traditions, has Ancestors who help him with that, and is not opposing what it means to be a Wedigo.<br /> <br /> I mean it could be semantics, but a stereotype isn't a bad thing, it is a term that allows to easily convey a complex idea or matter. It is not the same as a prejudice or a clich\u00e9. So, when I say a Wendigo character should adhere (willingly) to the Wendigo stereotype than I mean, the character should be recognizable as Wendigo in what he does, says, opinions he has and maybe even cloth he wears. That is far from adhering to a stereotype to a degree that it becomes a clich\u00e9.<br /> <br /> My point being, if you want to play a Wendigo, then play your character with all the positive and negative aspects that come with such a complex idea. Finding exceptions or downplaying or even ignoring aspects might be okay for a troupe, if everyone agrees to it. But if the ST (or the other players) feel that it goes to far, it probably did. Roleplaying is about playing a role, and not necessarily picking the role apart and take all the fun stuff and leave the difficult stuff behind.<br /> That makes the difference between stories driven solely by ST generated exterior motivators, and player character driven problems, that can also be fun.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-28T01:36:58-08:00","id":818288,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=818274#post818274\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Basically the 2nd paragraph's second sentence.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except you're not...<br /> <br /> The issue seems to be semantic, but not that stereotypes are inherently positive or negative, but that you're conflating stereotypes with the cultural norms that spawn them.<br /> <br /> My point is that players should play to the Tribal culture and ideology of their characters, not the stereotypes of those concepts. And... you seem to mostly just be saying that you disagree, despite then saying your position is essentially the exact same position but replacing &quot;stereotype&quot; with &quot;clich\u00e9&quot; and &quot;cultural norm,&quot; with &quot;stereotype.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My point being, if you want to play a Wendigo, then play your character with all the positive and negative aspects that come with such a complex idea. </div> </div> </div>I disagree with this solely on the grounds that one character cannot truly explore the totality of something as complex as a Tribal identity. There's no compelling reason to call for players and STs to not allow individual characters to focus on specific aspects of Tribal culture and identity, either in forum-theory, or at the game table. It also ends up hanging too much on one aspect of a character. Having a character who's relation to their Tribe's culture is pretty much all positives doesn't mean the player is inherently avoiding negative character aspects; they can come from other places like tensions between personality and Auspice duties, or Breed issues.<br /> <br /> The sum total of the character matters in regards to making a well-rounded character with interesting positive and negative aspects, not just one thing like Tribe selection.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-28T01:50:29-08:00","id":818292,"title":"","content":"I like cookies. Getting the recipe right, using shapes to cut them and define their edges, that joy of the senses when they are baked, done and ready to enjoy. But what I really like, is adding things like chocolate chips, salted caramel pieces, maybe some nuts or just adding a few sprinkles on top. Still the same shape, still the same dough mix but with a little something for flavor and a hint of a personal touch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7706-somuchlove","name":"Somuchlove","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7706_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-28T02:32:40-08:00","id":818305,"title":"","content":"The problem here is those examples. They aren't even stereotypes. A Get who shows mercy? The Get Stereotype is being a bit intollerant and doesn't step down from a fight. Shadowlords are pragmatic and cunning while they have no place for weaknesses that hinder, overcoming them is as much a pragmatic benefit for the group as anything else. I'd laugh at an ST who considered those problems.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-28T02:35:51-08:00","id":818307,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Somuchlove</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=818292#post818292\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like cookies. Getting the recipe right, using shapes to cut them and define their edges, that joy of the senses when they are baked, done and ready to enjoy. But what I really like, is adding things like chocolate chips, salted caramel pieces, maybe some nuts or just adding a few sprinkles on top. Still the same shape, still the same dough mix but with a little something for flavor and a hint of a personal touch.</div> </div> </div> </div>Way better than the cookie dough analogy from the Buffy finale.<br /> <br /> In any case, it comes down to how far one strays from the original recipe of being a certain tribe. Obviously a personal touch is okay, for not all characters are the same, but once the result you get is not recognizable as cookie anymore, NPCs should intervene, in an appropriate manner.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-28T05:47:44-08:00","id":818360,"title":"","content":"Heinrich puts it well when he speaks of culture rather then stereotypes. I feel this is something that requires deeper exploration.<br /> <br /> Why do we even have tribal stereotypes? It's a crutch made to simply explain to new players and storytellers what the Garou nation is right. These Stereotypes as presented in the two page writeups are so broad and hard to peg down, that it's quite easy to misrepresent and misinterpret the tribes from there.<br /> <br /> What tribes are, are cultural groups. They all have elaborate history and cultural norms. There is weight, supported by oral tradition and spirits that were there to live the greatest achievements of the tribe. All of these Tribes are tied in a common religion whose basis and Truth is reaffirmed every single day. We do not define Jews by their stereotype, nor do we expect Jews to act according the stereotype.<br /> <br /> This is true for the Tribes as well. They have their traditions and expectations, but first and foremost they are human analogues. They have desires and dreams, they agree more or less with their elders, and even if they are brought up in similar conditions with similar expectations not all of them will be posterboys of their tribe. As long as this is reasonable* there is no reason to sanction this outside the game. Inside the game the consequences will be obvious, and there will be conflict and lessons to learn and teach. And that's kinda the point!<br /> <br /> And that is fine. Every shapeshifter, especially a PC shapeshifter is a character first and foremost.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:8px\">* There are people for who comical idiotic characters are essentially in character soapbox for their players to fight the injustice and horror that is the setting of Werewolf</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-29T06:14:40-08:00","id":819165,"title":"","content":"Well, game mechanics are outside the game. Renown loss, or lack of gain, is a out-of-game sanction, I find suitable. Also, not allowing character concepts, that blatantly oppose the tribes ideals. Sure, everything is justifiable, with a more or less convincing background story, doesn't make it fitting the Setting or mood of the game, though.<br /> <br /> And like I said, stereotype isn't a negative thing. Stereotypes don't have to be false assumptions, they might just as well be accurate. And talking about the stereotypes of fictional tribes, the probably are. Stereotypes might form due to a bias, or a perceived correlation, that isn't real - so these stereotypes aren't accurate, too. But some are based on general observations, that are true for a certain group's majority. And with the need to identify with the peers in one's own group, one is usually inclined to mimic their behaviour. So for outside observers this leads to form a stereotype, that is in fact a attribute that is accurate for the group in question in most cases or under most circumstances.<br /> <br /> But, to distinguish a stereotypical &quot;Shadowlord&quot; from a Shadowlord, who is exhibiting characteristics other tribes would expect due to their prejudices, one should probably call the stereotypical one the norm-&quot;Schadowlord&quot;.<br /> <br /> It is all a definition thing.<br /> <br /> In any case, straying from the norm of the tribe should meet sanctions, if it excesses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-09T09:05:27-08:00","id":826866,"title":"","content":"Recently I created a Tengu for a play by forum game. Since I like mythology and I do not like so much the WtA bacground for changing breeds, I didn't make my Tengu as a nosy girl who could not keep her mouth shut. In fact, she was a diplomatic, and quite prudent, close to the ideal Japanese personality.<br /> <br /> My Storyteller asked me for these, and I explained to him. He told me that it was ok, but that he would like to see that in some occasions some information would escape from her mouth, surprising even her. We agreed.<br /> <br /> I think this is how these situation should be addressed. Just ask your players about why they are roleplaying like that, and if they want to have a merciful Fenris or compassionate Shadow Lord and you don't like that, tell them and try to come to a tradeoff.<br /> <br /> PS: If you want to encourage tribal stereotypes there is a system in which you can reward with more Renown a character when the feat represents positive qualities of the tribe, and punish with less Renown when the infraction is forgivable for their negative stereotypes (i.e. a Shadowlord would lose less Renown for betraying someone).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3673-mu_","name":"Mu_","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3673_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-25T19:30:48-08:00","id":837359,"title":"","content":"(First and foremost, I have to point out that it's not necessarily breaking any stereotypes for the Fenrir to show mercy, as the Litany demands that Garou accept an honorable surrender.)<br /> <br /> Honestly, it depends on the tribe, and it depends on the reasons.<br /> <br /> Some of the tribes would appear very &quot;old school&quot; to us. There is a strain of thought that has slowly been diminished in the modern world that conformity is survival. The Fenrir, Silver Fangs, and Shadow Lords would be the least accepting of people straying from the &quot;typical&quot; tribe member, and mostly for practical reasons. Old-World Fianna as well, but less so as you get further out.<br /> <br /> For tribes like the Bone Gnawers, Children of Gaia, Silent Striders, and even the Stargazers, I get the sense that their members are given much more freedom to live as they choose, and that any conformity to tribal stereotypes would rely more on the cultural or ethnic origin of the Garou in question, and possibly the demands and drawbacks of their tribal totem.<br /> <br /> If we're going to get into auspice, obviously Ragabash have the easiest means of justifying their need to break stereotypes, but that shouldn't be a blank check to do whatever the player chooses. Philodox should be the most conservative when it comes to tribal stereotypes due to their affinity for law and custom, while Ahroun would most likely be a close second mostly due to their straightforward nature. Theurge and Galliard get a little wiggle room for being weird and expressive, respectively, although a Theurge may also be more conservative due to their intimate understanding of relationships with spirits, especially the demands of their tribal totem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7445-viking-funeral","name":"Viking Funeral","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7445_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-26T00:44:57-08:00","id":837426,"title":"","content":"One could argue, that it ain't mercy if the surrender is honourable. It would be, if the opponent is to proud or to foolish, thickheaded to surrender. If the law requires you to accept the surrender, than you doing so isn't a selfless act and therefore per definition not mercy (see wikipedia: &quot;To be mercy, the behavior generally can not be compelled by outside forces.&quot;)<br /> <br /> But I think that tribal stereotypes go deeper than &quot;conformity is survival&quot;. They formed, like any characteristics any given culture has, as a means to demonstrate a shared identity and togetherness. They fulfill the need everyone feels more or less strongly to identify with some group and belong. So, even lost cubs, who didn't get raised with all the values and traditions of the tribe they enter, surely adapt their ways rather fast just to satisfy this need. And sure, this need can be evolutionarily explained as &quot;group means survival&quot;, but still it is more than just that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-29T17:08:03-08:00","id":839547,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=837426#post837426\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One could argue, that it ain't mercy if the surrender is honourable. It would be, if the opponent is to proud or to foolish, thickheaded to surrender. If the law requires you to accept the surrender, than you doing so isn't a selfless act and therefore per definition not mercy (see wikipedia: &quot;To be mercy, the behavior generally can not be compelled by outside forces.&quot;)</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=837426#post837426\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But I think that tribal stereotypes go deeper than &quot;conformity is survival&quot;. They formed, like any characteristics any given culture has, as a means to demonstrate a shared identity and togetherness. They fulfill the need everyone feels more or less strongly to identify with some group and belong. So, even lost cubs, who didn't get raised with all the values and traditions of the tribe they enter, surely adapt their ways rather fast just to satisfy this need. And sure, this need can be evolutionarily explained as &quot;group means survival&quot;, but still it is more than just that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Most definitely. That's an excellent way to describe it across the tribes. When referring to those tribes, I only meant to describe additional cultural forces that would lead them to hold that attitude. The Silver Fangs are bound by tradition more than probably any other tribe, and it wouldn't be too far outside the stereotype for the Fenrir to hold an attitude of group conformity similar to a military unit or a sports team.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7445-viking-funeral","name":"Viking Funeral","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7445_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-27T00:23:41-07:00","id":875396,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=818146#post818146\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am fine if a PC wants to buck the tribal culture, but I always stress that the NPCs will react accordingly. In these kind of cultures, the nail that sticks up gets hammered down. The character may be socially isolated, not considered respectable, looked down upon, and this may have an impact on Renown rewards.<br /> <br /> Some players are fine with this, and even get a kick out of it. To compensate for the in-character burdens, I may boost their XP award for roleplaying, but not the extent other PCs get turned off. However, in experience most players who want to buck tribal culture don't do it to experience hardship and drama. It's because they want to be treated like special snowflakes or even get praised for &quot;reforming&quot; their tribal culture. They get upset when the NPCs don't react the way they imagined. So I am very blunt about it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. How their actions and stuff show/are seen, is what will cause npcs to react to them differently. They might need to do some more brownnosing to get that rite performed, or before asking for that favor.. Or someone higher up, might actually like that the young cub is not acting like a 'typical XYZ..'..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-27T07:16:45-07:00","id":875599,"title":"","content":"People seem to be forgetting that the stereotypes presented in WW Books are <i>misconceptions outsiders have about other groups</i> that don't necessarily have <i>anything</i> to do with those groups in general, let alone individuals within that group.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-27T07:29:55-07:00","id":875614,"title":"","content":"Not necessarily. 'A <b>stereotype</b> is a thought that can be adopted about specific types of individuals or certain ways of doing things. These thoughts or beliefs may or may not accurately reflect <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reality\" target=\"_blank\">reality</a>.', so tells us wikipedia.<br /> <br /> The boxes detailing, what a member of a tribe usually thinks the other tribes, as presented on the write-ups for tribes (or in the tribe books) might contain misconceptions, but the whole write-up or tribe book conveys a stereotype about the tribe to the reader, and these aren't misconceptions. Or, at least not completely - since the tribe books are written in-character for a large part, they are somewhat unreliable sources...<br /> <br /> Thing is, there are some fundamental aspects to the culture (and therefore behaviour, religion, etc.) of a given tribe, that defines it as a tribe, and these are the stereotypes, or archetypes, a character from that tribe should adhere to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-27T08:53:55-07:00","id":875694,"title":"","content":"Wee necros.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I agree strongly with heinrich's point about stereotypes not being inherently misconceptions (they're preconceptions) and a good number of the stereotypes presented out-of-character in the books are intended to simplify Tribal cultural to summarize them for introduction rather than be misleading.<br /> <br /> I'll reaffirm my objection to his last point though, as again, expecting any one character to adhere to every major aspect of a Trbie's cultural identity is absurd. It is impossible to distill any of the Tribes fundamental aspects down to a point where every canon example character adheres to each of those aspects; let alone characters we create. The books clearly present characters that care far more about adhering to some of those aspects than others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-27T09:43:19-07:00","id":875731,"title":"","content":"Well, &quot;should adhere to&quot; as opposed to &quot;must adhere to&quot;. My problem is usually with characters who want to build characters that are of a given tribe/culture, but oppose one or more fundamental (in my opinion) aspects of that tribe/culture - for (in my opinion) no other reason than being a special snowflake.<br /> I usually have no problem with characters that tone down or ignore one such aspect, in favour of other aspects of the tribe (or the character, as breed or auspice aspects).<br /> <br /> And, for obvious reasons (namely LARP), I have to maintain a coherent game world with a lot of players than an average troupe. Characters that are opposing the tribe/culture they claim to belong all too often create problems for the other players who have characters of that tribe and that is a game element they usually do not appreciate. And for the player who has the opposing character it is generating game exposure on the expanse of the other players, and that is not fair either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-27T12:30:15-07:00","id":875850,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=875731#post875731\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, &quot;should adhere to&quot; as opposed to &quot;must adhere to&quot;. My problem is usually with characters who want to build characters that are of a given tribe/culture, but oppose one or more fundamental (in my opinion) aspects of that tribe/culture - for (in my opinion) no other reason than being a special snowflake.<br /> I usually have no problem with characters that tone down or ignore one such aspect, in favour of other aspects of the tribe (or the character, as breed or auspice aspects).</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. Flat out ignoring (or trying to get around) certain aspects of a tribe/auspice/breed just cause they can often shows bad form, such as for say a ADND game mages consistently wanting to have weapons so the can fight, or clerics who refuse to heal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-27T12:36:30-07:00","id":875860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=875731#post875731\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, &quot;should adhere to&quot; as opposed to &quot;must adhere to&quot;. My problem is usually with characters who want to build characters that are of a given tribe/culture, but oppose one or more fundamental (in my opinion) aspects of that tribe/culture - for (in my opinion) no other reason than being a special snowflake.<br /> I usually have no problem with characters that tone down or ignore one such aspect, in favour of other aspects of the tribe (or the character, as breed or auspice aspects).</div> </div> </div> </div>To this, I note one interesting viewpoint I have heard about Rite of Passages. That you could join a tribe without much education of it, aside from a mentor. Or even that the totem the character meets at the end is not what was expected but another more fitting one.<br /> <br /> With these characters, I highly doubt tribal culture was enforced heavily. Good example is the revised Wendigo theurge template, where the theurge is a lupus avoiding other garou by being a librarian.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-27T15:14:31-07:00","id":876041,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=875731#post875731\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, &quot;should adhere to&quot; as opposed to &quot;must adhere to&quot;. My problem is usually with characters who want to build characters that are of a given tribe/culture, but oppose one or more fundamental (in my opinion) aspects of that tribe/culture - for (in my opinion) no other reason than being a special snowflake.</div> </div> </div> </div>First there's an issue of your general language conflating &quot;opposed to&quot; and &quot;tone down&quot; in your previous posts.<br /> <br /> Second, the problem here is, &quot;no other reason than being a special snowflake,&quot; not the actual idea of making a character that opposes an aspect of the Tribe's culture while still belonging to it.<br /> <br /> I appreciate that what makes a concept a special snowflake is variable - and larger group play like LARPs require a tighter control on concepts - but you can make a character that's interesting and in-theme that's opposed to such things without it always being a special snowflake issue. Otherwise it would be impossible to explore one of the themes of the game of the present &quot;young&quot; generation of Garou being dissatisfied with the traditional ways of their people and wanting to change the way things are done. Playing an iconoclast isn't inherently a bad thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T00:30:55-07:00","id":876326,"title":"","content":"If such a character is consistent. I mean I can't see many tribes who would allow for a garou who openly opposes key aspects of the tribe to start, yet alone survive a Rite of Passage. I read that most garou who grow dissatisfied with the ways of their tribes become Ronin.<br /> Unlike vampire clans, blood isn't everything that is required to be of a certain tribe - people who don't (want to) fit is are expelled, or at least remove them selves from that culture and go on to live somewhere else.<br /> I can see that a more or less merciless Child of Gaia would leave and life with the Get of Fenris before the tribe casts her out, as an example.<br /> <br /> I guess one needs to want to ST this kind of stories or allow for them to happen parallel to the story one intended. For such character can fit into some stories, but usually they don't fit in septs of the tribe they (partly) oppose.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T01:35:00-07:00","id":876360,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/817404-st-how-do-you-handle-it-when-characters-stray-from-tribal-stereotypes?p=876326#post876326\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If such a character is consistent. I mean I can't see many tribes who would allow for a garou who openly opposes key aspects of the tribe to start, yet alone survive a Rite of Passage. I read that most garou who grow dissatisfied with the ways of their tribes become Ronin.<br /> Unlike vampire clans, blood isn't everything that is required to be of a certain tribe - people who don't (want to) fit is are expelled, or at least remove them selves from that culture and go on to live somewhere else.<br /> I can see that a more or less merciless Child of Gaia would leave and life with the Get of Fenris before the tribe casts her out, as an example.</div> </div> </div> </div>As I noted above, there is an idea that you only need to be accepted by the totem to be part of the tribe. And that Rite of Passages can happen without in-depth tribal training at a tribal sept.<br /> <br /> But there is also the fact that not all garou of a tribe do act according to the tribal ways. The easiest example is a Fianna metis, despite being accepted by Stag, they are not known for mirthful storytelling or partying. As they are not allowed by the tribe itself. Same with Silver Fang metis. Then there are Children of Gaia who are merciless due to the Burned Out flaw, from being rejected one too many times.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T02:00:03-07:00","id":876370,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1008_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1008\" data-vbnamecard=\"1008\">heinrich</a> <br /> <br /> The problem is that you're treating Tribes as monolithic in their cultural natures; despite the clear evidence this isn't true. The Tribes all have Camps which show a large amount of philosophic variation in the Tribes. The books tell us that the Tribes are full of internal conflicts over which aspects of their Tribe are most important, who's the &quot;truest&quot; expression of Tribal values, and so on.<br /> <br /> Painting it such that Tribes are essentially hive-minds where everyone must conform or they're killed/cast out as Ronin is not how the books state things are, not something that's logical about the nature of Tribes, and something that sounds incredibly boring. The way you talk about Tribes, they're not what the books seem to be talking about, they're fanatical cults where non-conformity is clearly worse than any Litany violation.<br /> <br /> You're also ignoring the huge number of other factors that play into Tribal membership; such as breeding/parentage, the scarcity of the Garou necessitating numbers over vague ideological bickering, and as Ana Mizuki mentioned the role of the Tribal Totems and associated spirits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T02:57:37-07:00","id":876412,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure how common or uncommon certain camps within a given tribe is, and surely some tribes are more tolerant than others. I'm not saying that each tribe has for every topic one narrow standpoint from what that they may never diverge. Obviously there is a bandwidth of opinions for every issue that is acceptable for each tribe, but there are still opinions that are not. And certainly not meeting the tribally acceptable bandwidth in one area doesn't call for expulsion from the tribe. How good one fits into a tribe is more of a multi-dimensional correlation type of situation.<br /> <br /> And sure, breeding/parenting is certainly a factor for tribal membership (for some tribes more than others). If one argues that the tribe should not turn down an applicant due to the scarcity of garou in general, someone else will comment, that the applicant should go to a &quot;catch-all&quot; tribe like Children of Gaia or Bone Gnawers but not be allowed to weaken the Get, Fianna, Silver Fangs, or any more traditional tribe with his sub-tribal-par characteristics. Catch-all being a exaggeration, for these tribes have an in-game reputation for having a broad bandwidth of what is acceptable, that other tribes don't have.<br /> <br /> &quot;Cynicism and callousness are increasingly common, and the numbers of ronin (Garou who turn away from their pack and tribe) swell with each new moon.&quot; (from Werewolf 2nd) kind of tells us a little what the fate of a garou with &quot;Burn Out&quot; flaw might be. Sure, here is a shift in the Setting from different tribes competing for mundane and spiritual resources in present day to revised's working together attitude.<br /> <br /> As for Tribal Totems: well, I see the chicken/egg problem here. Does/should a totem give clear instruction to let a garou into the tribe, no questions asked? If not, if the instructions are vague, vision-like, prophecy-ridden and what not, don't the garou have an obligation to check if the interpreted them right? Also, wouldn't due to the tribe/totem-symbioses the standards the tribe enforces be the same the Totem enforces? So, would the totem maybe only intervene, if such qualities weren't obvious in the cub to the garou and the totem wants to give them a hint?<br /> <br /> Prophecies or divine intervention make nice story elements, but I don't think it is an all to common occurrence. And they are a kind of thought-terminating, if garou in game would rely on determining who is fit for the tribe an who not, why educate, and why have tribal specific Rites of Passage? Obviously, as a LARP-ST, with a lot of players starting as cubs I detest that players of a given tribe shift off the responsibility to judge whether or not a young garou is fitting for their tribe to the Totem aka the Storytellers.<br /> <br /> And obviously there is a mention of a Get of Fenris, Fenris and the garou who educated, trained and trailed (Rite of Passage) him accepted, despite the fact that he was a reincarnated Stargazer soul who once Fostern left the tribe an joined the Stargazers. So, what a Totem allows and what not, in the game world is vague. Of Evan-heals-the-Past, who was declared Wendigo by ancestor spirits, it is said that for the first years he still was constantly tested by all Wendigo elders he met for the first time. And as a high ranking theurge in Wendigo TN paraphrased Spirits are unreliable, ever changing and contradicting, also circumstances change and visions are misinterpreted. One has to form one's own opinion an make one's own decisions.&quot;<br /> <br /> In the end, it depends on the tone of the game of werewolf you want to play/ST. Certainly some games can or should allow more leeway with the characteristics a tribe member should present than other games. One has to find a level the troupe is comfortable with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T10:22:54-07:00","id":876774,"title":"","content":"Very interesting discussion!<br /> <br /> I handle it in a fair way, i create a NPC that is fairly stereotypical and let them have success that is at least similar to the one that the PC has. He most likely actually likes the PC, but other tribe member might - from time to time and without being facetious - ask the PC why he is not more like the NPC or when he fails he gets things to hear like &quot;Even NPC makes mistakes sometimes&quot;. Very effective and it helps the character actually play said member of a tribe. <br /> <br /> Contrasting your PC with such a NPC will make the player invested in him. Maybe he will unconsciously start to dislike him and try to denounce the NPC or they become strong friends but the player will engage with that PC and it will strengthen the chronicle. <br /> <br /> From my experience that is way more effective than have other tribes member being consistently being negative towards the PC.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8249-crytash","name":"Crytash","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8249_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"386"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T23:45:56-07:00","id":877399,"title":"","content":"I like that. Positive reinforcement or an example to look up to might be a good way to influence the player. It is also what should happen when a character is a cub and learns the ways of the tribe. In tabletop games it also is easier to incorporate such a NPC into the game and it makes the sept live more realistic and interesting.<br /> It is hard, though, if a player has set his mind on doing it differently.<br /> <br /> Also, in LARP game this approach is far more difficult, for there aren't a lot of NPCs around to fill such a position, and while the other players could fill such a role (and should) it is not really fair to change plots so that they succeed or are respected for their commitment to tribal ways.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T01:12:58-07:00","id":877453,"title":"","content":"That is absolutely true. Funny enough i am soon running a cub to legend game for table top and am creating some plot lines that are important to &quot;teach&quot; the player characters if need be. Obviously this technic can be used for other game lines as well and is used in a story i play as a PC.<br /> <br /> As for LARP, you would have to &quot;use&quot; a PC to do the job which is inherently more difficult and imo leads to infighting and drama.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8249-crytash","name":"Crytash","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8249_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"386"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}